{"context":"Provenance is specified and implemented, but no field expresses when knowledge stops being trustworthy and no query answers whether an artifact is stale. The damaging case is an approved artifact at a high stratum describing a subject that has since changed: because lower strata must not contradict higher, a stale high-stratum artifact misleads with institutional authority. `Updated` is file modification time, which says when the file was touched, not whether the content still holds.","decision":"Add an additive validity horizon: two optional envelope fields (`validUntil`, `lastConfirmed`) holding absolute dates, authored by hand with no default, carried through the exchange Unit and the machine Document via omitempty so default documents stay byte-identical. Freshness is advisory only — conformance never compares to a wall clock, because that would make verdicts time-dependent and rot every long-lived corpus. The query surface is a `--freshness` flag on the existing retrieval command plus one line in `eka status`, not a new verb. Evidence beyond the declared horizon is out of scope for v1. The rationale and the rejected alternatives for each of these six questions are recorded in detail in the design document this decision supersedes.","consequences":"Staleness becomes expressible without breaking any existing corpus and without a migration: absent fields mean undeclared, which is an honest third status rather than a false claim of currency. The cost is adoption — an optional field that is never written is inert — so visibility in the existing retrieval path is the only mechanism that makes it useful. The ordering constraint matters more than the volume of change: the conformance artifact field and the JSON top-level allow-list must land together, because a field the JSON adapter rejects is worse than no field.","alternativesConsidered":"A duration re-evaluated against the last update was rejected: update is file modification time, so a touch would silently extend validity forever. A per-type default horizon was rejected because a wrong default manufactures false confidence in both directions and is worse than no default. Blocking conformance was rejected: it makes verdicts depend on wall-clock time, breaks reproducibility, and contradicts the precedent that refusal is reserved for structural violations rather than editorial judgement. A new `eka stale` verb was rejected in favour of a flag on the existing retrieval surface. Deriving staleness from `file:` references was deferred because that reference grammar does not exist in the rule set."}